# bp-migrate.service — Prisma migrations against the hosted Supabase database.
#
# This is a plain systemd unit, NOT a Quadlet .container, and NOT part of
# bp-monitor.target. Both of those are deliberate.
#
# ── Why migrations are not run on container start ───────────────────────────
# In the Compose stack, Postgres was a container on the same network and its
# lifetime was the stack's lifetime. Against a hosted database, none of that
# holds:
#
#   - The gateway container has Restart=always. A migrate-on-boot step turns a
#     crash loop into repeated schema-mutation attempts against the production
#     database.
#   - `prisma migrate deploy` takes a Postgres advisory lock. Two containers
#     starting at once (a redeploy overlapping a restart) race for it, and the
#     loser can time out mid-migration.
#   - The blast radius is no longer a container volume you can delete. It is a
#     hosted database with real patient readings in it.
#
# So: migrations are an explicit operator action, taken once per deploy that
# ships one, in the window the operator chose. Run it BEFORE starting the
# gateway image that requires the new schema (additive schema, then code, then
# a separate later change for any drop):
#
#   systemctl --user start bp-migrate.service
#   journalctl --user -u bp-migrate.service -n 100 --no-pager
#
# It is Type=oneshot, so `start` blocks until it finishes and a non-zero exit
# leaves the unit failed and visible in `systemctl --user --failed`.
#
# ── Why a different DATABASE_URL ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supabase's transaction-mode pooler (port 6543) multiplexes client connections
# onto fewer server connections and cannot hold a session across statements.
# Prisma's migrate engine needs session-scoped state: an advisory lock held for
# the length of the migration, and DDL in a transaction. Run against the
# transaction pooler it either errors out or, worse, takes a lock that does not
# mean what it thinks it means.
#
# So this unit injects a DIFFERENT secret — bp-database-url-direct — over the
# same DATABASE_URL variable name. That name reuse is what makes this work with
# no application change: server/app/api-gateway/prisma.config.ts reads
# `process.env.DATABASE_URL` for the migrate datasource, so pointing the
# variable somewhere else for the length of this one container run is enough.
#
# bp-database-url-direct must be either the direct endpoint
# (db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432) or the SESSION-mode pooler
# (<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432). Not 6543. On an IPv4-only EC2 subnet the
# direct endpoint may be unreachable — Supabase's direct hostname is IPv6-only
# for projects without the IPv4 add-on — in which case the session pooler on
# 5432 is the one that works. Verify with `getent hosts` before assuming.
#
# ── What runs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `prisma migrate deploy` — never `migrate dev`, which is interactive, can
# reset, and can generate new migration files. The prisma CLI is present in the
# api-gateway prod image because the Dockerfile installs the full dependency
# tree (no --prod flag on pnpm install).
#
# BP_IMAGE_TAG comes from /etc/bp-monitor/bp-monitor.env and must be the SAME
# tag you are about to deploy. Migrating with one image and running another is
# how a schema and the code that expects it get out of step.

[Unit]
Description=BP Monitor — Prisma migrate deploy (manual gate, Supabase)
Documentation=file:///opt/bp-monitor/infra/podman/README.md
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=no
EnvironmentFile=/etc/bp-monitor/bp-monitor.env

ExecStart=/usr/bin/podman run --rm --name bp-migrate \
    --network bp-net \
    --secret bp-database-url-direct,type=env,target=DATABASE_URL \
    --memory=512m --cpus=0.5 \
    localhost/bp-monitor/api-gateway:${BP_IMAGE_TAG} \
    pnpm prisma migrate deploy

# A failed migration must be loud and must not be retried automatically.
Restart=no
TimeoutStartSec=600
